\chapter{Methods}
\section{Surveillance data}
- data used: HealthData.gov COVID-19 Reported Patient Impact and Hospital Capacity by State Time Series (reports daily incident hospitalizations for all 50 states, several US territories, and the AGGREGATED US national level)
- dataset sources: HHSTeleTracking, state/territorial health departments which report to HHS Protect, and National Health Care Safety Network (prior to July 15, 2020). Note that the first date of truth data used is July 27, 2020 (see Date range of analysis)
- This data set designated official ground truth data for COVID-19 incident hospitalizations on December 1, 2020 by the US COVID-19 Forecast Hub [https://covid19forecast-hub.org/], for usage as both input training data and to compare against for accuracy evaluations
- hospitalization reporting sporadic for first four months of pandemic, consistency only achieved for all locations in late July 2020 (all territories designated "states" had consistent reporting starting [date])
- Hospitalization data became more reliable compared to case and death data due to reporting for Medicare reimbursement. Case and death data have suffering major under reporting issues that only grew worse as the pandemic continued on
- Truth data collection for COVID-19 incident cases and incident deaths was discontinued in March 2023, with John Hopkins University (the official ground truth data source for those targets) shutting down their COVID dashboard(?)
- Note though that hospitalization data is not perfect and values may be updated, especially within the first few weeks and incident hospitalization value is posted. However, models are training on the currently available data to them, which may not reflect the finalized truth value
- Truth data is queried through Zoltar, a data repository set up by..., which includes past versions of the truth data so that original values may be obtained. The data used to train the models in this analysis is queried such that it would be the data available as of the date that the retrospective forecast is "made on" so as to be comparable to other models.

